-9509-5358-a245-a13ba912d2df" :question/math-academy-id "q-81229" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}+8{b}_{2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+8(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81229/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81229"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80835/selection/6fef27588dc6a2a540993b0bf71ee90192ce080df3a3686c50cf68384c373aef" :answer/id #uuid "e18c26d7-ea65-5c8c-8610-d8a8c32c6390" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80835/selection/b6347a4d6792ce7c0956c19ada0917ffbc35a9f5e86b5249cbcff828d07be379" :answer/id #uuid "ca0dc37d-b770-5eef-9e6c-e5b8db427989" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80835/selection/b33248d24ebcae43ba434d876bc6ce10a3b8aab4caad090b8905414496b541a2" :answer/id #uuid "8e4514f6-a1fb-51f5-a325-c1d8923a512b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80835/selection/b8f9154fa51e156272457c63e50c57122b695148952f6ac52a11620a577db463" :answer/id #uuid "699cda3c-4767-5ff1-b3ee-65ded4807f96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80835/selection/005f9eb3b9aad01ed080ab3a957430e26817ac1b43294ee44aa49d9659736e7c" :answer/id #uuid "b8f50825-c3b3-55f8-8e83-bdeeb17796e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80835/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "34a98386-b9ef-5b87-bb5a-1d645bb64037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80835/selection/b33248d24ebcae43ba434d876bc6ce10a3b8aab4caad090b8905414496b541a2" :answer-field/choices ["q-80835/selection/6fef27588dc6a2a540993b0bf71ee90192ce080df3a3686c50cf68384c373aef" "q-80835/selection/b6347a4d6792ce7c0956c19ada0917ffbc35a9f5e86b5249cbcff828d07be379" "q-80835/selection/b33248d24ebcae43ba434d876bc6ce10a3b8aab4caad090b8905414496b541a2" "q-80835/selection/b8f9154fa51e156272457c63e50c57122b695148952f6ac52a11620a577db463" "q-80835/selection/005f9eb3b9aad01ed080ab3a957430e26817ac1b43294ee44aa49d9659736e7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80835" :question/id #uuid "fda7b684-3b39-5fc1-88de-29fdd05dbc36" :question/math-academy-id "q-80835" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $-1$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{2}-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+({c}_{2}-{c}_{1})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})+({c}_{2}-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}+{c}_{2}-{c}_{1} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-80835/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80835"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112833/selection/028155ad1d27a47ba71667327fd1ba19e1125decdfcf259ca7daa760a56a0e3f" :answer/id #uuid "2adad20a-504e-5d34-b389-576567d86afe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112833/selection/a52ab47e5e1e1a211361d1ab5f743041cc5ecf7eebb977653902e7529396c119" :answer/id #uuid "fa32bfe0-9f0f-51ef-9dc2-eaa683e902c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112833/selection/b2578655faa68cb660f58a94be72bfe99f40d4cbd38759bdfdffd3f1416d7180" :answer/id #uuid "2a7b0da2-7aed-5e46-aaa2-344f2582d8d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112833/selection/6b4682df80091df2d1b024310c2fa921796c8473f258f04ae912621b044f6c6c" :answer/id #uuid "6ea2c2be-255b-5ea3-844b-c0942c90108f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112833/selection/31b8449ec2e042bd59318a714846be304eb717ec52fa375d6d41e4fc5acb18d7" :answer/id #uuid "3bf65cc1-afa3-5a54-9df2-f2c375e18a21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112833/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe1fc2b8-045e-53b7-8101-56a310e1299b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112833/selection/028155ad1d27a47ba71667327fd1ba19e1125decdfcf259ca7daa760a56a0e3f" :answer-field/choices ["q-112833/selection/028155ad1d27a47ba71667327fd1ba19e1125decdfcf259ca7daa760a56a0e3f" "q-112833/selection/a52ab47e5e1e1a211361d1ab5f743041cc5ecf7eebb977653902e7529396c119" "q-112833/selection/b2578655faa68cb660f58a94be72bfe99f40d4cbd38759bdfdffd3f1416d7180" "q-112833/selection/6b4682df80091df2d1b024310c2fa921796c8473f258f04ae912621b044f6c6c" "q-112833/selection/31b8449ec2e042bd59318a714846be304eb717ec52fa375d6d41e4fc5acb18d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112833" :question/id #uuid "b1c7e82e-45cc-52c9-803f-d07a918acd1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112833" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-4{b}_{1}+5{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=-4{b}_{1}+5{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $5$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+5{c}_{2}=6{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-4(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2})+5{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{2}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2})-6(\\frac{2}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{6}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-112833/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112833"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80838/selection/6fef27588dc6a2a540993b0bf71ee90192ce080df3a3686c50cf68384c373aef" :answer/id #uuid "d53e3b0c-86e8-5373-9ec4-4d247f2f2d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80838/selection/eb2f1db174836d9822af2fba255e4457c267c94c7bbbe0906e94ad579c56415c" :answer/id #uuid "2b3a0628-0911-584f-a9e7-6166fff8cbe4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80838/selection/fbd6e5101541176b06eec087aa33baed199c39845adbfc2519c44f462da378b8" :answer/id #uuid "6c5691a1-f01a-593a-9f81-6885e8e7441a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80838/selection/125d8901168a03f7aeccdce39ea8fc27827279e54cee452c613599dce6f1a75f" :answer/id #uuid "12d7535c-540a-52a8-b25a-d57f1317f03d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80838/selection/e90068a4c392e2adaa4bd596692a19aac8c021a136f9eac0f451317494dccf7d" :answer/id #uuid "f750226d-f1d9-5666-9701-36d7b4700c40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80838/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b850130-9b94-533e-b114-2dec2846c1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80838/selection/fbd6e5101541176b06eec087aa33baed199c39845adbfc2519c44f462da378b8" :answer-field/choices ["q-80838/selection/6fef27588dc6a2a540993b0bf71ee90192ce080df3a3686c50cf68384c373aef" "q-80838/selection/eb2f1db174836d9822af2fba255e4457c267c94c7bbbe0906e94ad579c56415c" "q-80838/selection/fbd6e5101541176b06eec087aa33baed199c39845adbfc2519c44f462da378b8" "q-80838/selection/125d8901168a03f7aeccdce39ea8fc27827279e54cee452c613599dce6f1a75f" "q-80838/selection/e90068a4c392e2adaa4bd596692a19aac8c021a136f9eac0f451317494dccf7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80838" :question/id #uuid "05cca5db-1496-5858-9cd6-f4db1975d817" :question/math-academy-id "q-80838" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}-{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6{b}_{1}-{b}_{2} \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})-(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{8}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-80838/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80838"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4827"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/acda5be1511b340b3a947cbaa4dedfd7a3175029d2937481b261c7a0c4eb5348.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -\\mathbf{b}_2$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = -3\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/ac75a1bca2b8012016657f4d1d88fdf1725c84b2a3ec48376bbcb4670f134ba3.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =2(-{b}_{2})-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/d98192a7e16e605311ee2bb8791e6d86005c6d565ede80e68aaaeaf8097721a2.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4828"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/1e4b0e4345ef6d0bd0b350cc973eaa7c15b654e76b06cd6f42740af0ab8fc877.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/a5e125038beb90ff4fd2b16bea5b08f83855c80f2a70a73ccfc15fa53fd45a30.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2,$ and $\\mathbf c_3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3{b}_{2})-5(-{b}_{3})+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/f78654ee0612c6daa7e7a1fa26fb56ce0c3c94f6a7ba29805a569eac643946eb.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4829"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/aed9216ce4c536aad07f26eec0e0cb7e3032147a19c13e0a2d42925083c49518.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\mathbf{b}_2-\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = 2\\mathbf{b}_1 - 3\\mathbf{b}_2?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $\\mathbf{b}$'s in terms of $\\mathbf{c}$'s. Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2 = -\\mathbf{b}_2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\left( -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{b}_1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_1 = -3\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/80531bde3f2ebb6314a3a297efa44e3e21a33d0b5f628c67c39ef0fa288f4c78.png) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})-3(-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+6{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/f678c1ecc9878c30b04e891af9e6933f1edb42af8e155c3f4719838210e1cc1d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
